WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.176
Two retrievers, two lists, one answer.

2
00:00:02.456 --> 00:00:11.373
Last lesson ended with a finding that keeps recurring in the literature and in practice: keyword search and embedding search fail on different queries.

3
00:00:11.653 --> 00:00:18.267
BM25 nails “error TS-999” and misses “how do I get my money back”.

4
00:00:18.547 --> 00:00:20.061
Embeddings do the reverse.

5
00:00:20.341 --> 00:00:23.520
Hybrid search runs both and combines the results.

6
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.357
The architecture is simple.

7
00:00:25.637 --> 00:00:31.504
Each retriever returns its own top N candidates for the query, typically a few dozen to a hundred.

8
00:00:31.784 --> 00:00:34.771
A fusion step merges the lists into one ranking.

9
00:00:35.051 --> 00:00:40.448
The top k of that ranking goes on to a reranker (lesson 9) or straight into the prompt.

10
00:00:40.728 --> 00:00:52.589
Anthropic’s contextual retrieval post describes exactly this pipeline: retrieve by embeddings, retrieve by BM25, then combine and deduplicate using rank fusion.

11
00:00:52.869 --> 00:01:00.272
In their tests across several knowledge domains, embeddings plus BM25 retrieved better than embeddings alone.

12
00:01:00.552 --> 00:01:03.005
The interesting question is the fusion step.

13
00:01:03.285 --> 00:01:04.608
There are two families.

14
00:01:04.888 --> 00:01:08.963
Score fusion combines the retrievers’ scores, usually a weighted sum.

15
00:01:09.243 --> 00:01:12.443
Rank fusion ignores scores and combines positions.

16
00:01:12.723 --> 00:01:17.117
Each has a real argument behind it, and the choice matters more than it looks.
